%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 147873241f4f7964690daffb230f2761, type: 3}
  m_Name: Level3_TheDigitalTrap
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  levelId: level_3
  levelNumber: 3
  displayName: The Digital Trap
  sceneName: Room3_DigitalTrap
  description: What the padlock means, impostors asking for money, owning up to a
    mistake, second login checks, fake Wi-Fi and fake bank texts.
  previewImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: de67521957d88cb478527ddaa3a59230, type: 3}
  briefingTitle: "LEVEL 3 \u2014 THE DIGITAL TRAP"
  briefingText: 'In this room: what the padlock means, impostors asking for money,
    owning up to a mistake, second login checks, fake Wi-Fi and fake bank texts.

    You''ll
    get 6 questions: 5 with answer choices (A-D) and 1 to judge as SCAM or LEGITIMATE.
    Take your time and read each one carefully.'
  caseNotes:
  - 'Trusted domain (case files): examplebank.example'
  objectives:
  - Answer all 6 questions.
  - Read the whole situation before you choose. Stuck? Press HINT.
  - Score 590+ to unlock the next room.
  messages:
  - id: M03
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Multiple Choice
    body: A login page shows a padlock and starts with <b>https://</b>. <b>What does
      the padlock actually prove?</b>
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: The padlock (HTTPS) means your connection is private, so others
      can't read what you send. It doesn't say who owns the site. Anyone can get
      a padlock, scam sites too, so you still need to check the address.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: https
      field: 5
      match: https://
      label: https://
      finding: The 's' means the connection uses TLS encryption. Others on the network
        can't read or change your data.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: enc
      part: addr
    - id: lock
      field: 5
      match: Connection is secure
      label: The padlock
      finding: The connection is private - data in transit is encrypted. It says
        nothing about who runs the site.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: enc
      part: lock
    - id: cert
      field: 5
      match: controls this domain
      label: The certificate
      finding: Certificates are easy for anyone to get for their own domain - scam
        sites have padlocks too.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: anyone
      part: cert
    - id: form
      field: 5
      match: email and password
      label: The form
      finding: A padlock doesn't make a form safe to fill in. You still have to check
        the domain.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: form
    evidence:
    - id: enc
      title: Encrypted connection
      whyItMatters: That's what the padlock actually means.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: anyone
      title: Anyone can get a certificate
      whyItMatters: So a padlock can't prove the site is the real company.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - The site is owned by the real company
    - Data between you and <i>this</i> site is encrypted, but the site itself could
      still be fake
    - The site has been checked by the government
    - The site cannot contain malware
    correctChoice: 1
    feedback: Padlock = <b>private connection</b>, not <b>safe site</b>. Fake sites
      have padlocks too.
    curriculumRef: "Advanced Cybersecurity Strategies (BIT4233). Supporting: Computer
      Security (BIT1225), Cryptography \u2014 Modern Cryptography (\"secure data
      in transit and at rest\")"
    hint: A padlock is about the connection. Does it tell you who owns the site?
    activity:
      kind: 2
      title: Sign in
      instruction: Click the padlock. What does it really prove?
      items:
      - key: addr
        role: 0
        label: Address
        text: https://login-page.example/signin
      - key: lock
        role: 6
        label: click to view
        text: 'Padlock: Connection is secure'
      - key: cert
        role: 8
        label: 
        text: 'Certificate: issued to login-page.example. Checks that the requester
          controls this domain.'
      - key: form
        role: 1
        label: 
        text: Sign in with your email and password.
      minClues: 1
      outcomes: []
  - id: M04
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Identify the Threat
    body: "<b>SMS from an unknown number:</b> \"Ma, si Migs 'to. Nasira phone ko,
      bagong number ko 'to. Pa-send naman \u20B13,000 sa PayLite 0917 555 0188, emergency
      lang. Wag ka muna tumawag, wala pa akong load.\" <b>What attack is this?</b>\n<i>In
      English: \"Mom, it's Migs. My phone broke, this is my new number. Please send
      \u20B13,000 to PayLite 0917 555 0188, it's an emergency. Don't call yet, I
      have no load.\"</i>"
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 'A "family member" with a new number, a sudden emergency, a money
      request and "don''t call me" are classic signs. This trick is called <b>social
      engineering</b>: it uses your feelings, not hacking. Call Migs''s <i>old</i>
      number, or ask something only he would know.'
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: from
      field: 5
      match: not in your contacts
      label: Sender
      finding: Not in your contacts. The first text explains it away with a 'new
        number'.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: from
    - id: newnum
      field: 5
      match: bagong number ko 'to
      label: '"New number"'
      finding: Claiming to be family on a new number is how impersonators skip your
        contacts list.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: newnum
      part: m1
    - id: money
      field: 5
      match: "Pa-send naman \u20B13,000"
      label: The request
      finding: 'The whole story leads to one thing: send money now.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: money
      part: m2
    - id: urgent
      field: 5
      match: emergency lang
      label: Emergency
      finding: An invented emergency rushes you past checking.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: urgent
      part: m2
    - id: nocall
      field: 5
      match: Wag ka muna tumawag
      label: '"Don''t call yet"'
      finding: It stops you from calling Migs's old number - the easiest way to check.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: nocall
      part: m3
    evidence:
    - id: newnum
      title: Claims to be family on a new number
      whyItMatters: Impersonation exploits trust, not technology.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: money
      title: Asks for money
      whyItMatters: The goal of the story.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: urgent
      title: Invented emergency
      whyItMatters: Urgency stops you from thinking.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: nocall
      title: Blocks you from checking
      whyItMatters: Call the old number or ask something only Migs would know.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - 'Impersonation: someone pretending to be family with a "new number"'
    - Malware infection
    - A data breach
    - A promotional spam message
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: Impersonation! New number + emergency + "don't call" = <b>call the
      old number first.</b>
    curriculumRef: "Information Assurance in Security 1 (BIT1101) \u2014 Weeks 10\u201311:
      Cybersecurity Threats and Attack Landscape"
    hint: A new number, an emergency, a money request and "don't call me". How could
      you check it's really him?
    activity:
      kind: 3
      title: "Messages  \xB7  unknown number"
      instruction: Read the texts. Click each warning sign.
      items:
      - key: from
        role: 0
        label: From
        text: Unknown number (not in your contacts)
      - key: m1
        role: 2
        label: Unknown number
        text: Ma, si Migs 'to. Nasira phone ko, bagong number ko 'to.
      - key: m2
        role: 2
        label: Unknown number
        text: "Pa-send naman \u20B13,000 sa PayLite 0917 555 0188, emergency lang."
      - key: m3
        role: 2
        label: Unknown number
        text: Wag ka muna tumawag, wala pa akong load.
      minClues: 2
      outcomes: []
  - id: M05
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: What Should You Do?
    body: During your OJT, you typed your work password into a page you opened from
      an email. A minute later, you notice the address was wrong.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: Act fast. Changing your password makes the stolen one useless, and
      IT can block the fake site and warn others. Hiding it gives the attacker time.
      Forwarding spreads the bad link, and deleting removes the proof IT needs.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: domain
      field: 5
      match: login-verify.example
      label: The real domain
      finding: 'Read from the right: the real domain is login-verify.example - not
        your company''s. This was a fake page.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: fake
      part: addr
    - id: done
      field: 5
      match: Your account has been verified
      label: The message
      finding: Fake pages often say 'verified' so you don't worry. Nothing was verified
        - your password was collected.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: done
    - id: typed
      field: 5
      match: work password
      label: What you typed
      finding: The attacker now has your work password. Every minute counts.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: stolen
      part: typed
    - id: email
      field: 5
      match: The email that sent you to this page
      label: The email
      finding: IT needs this email to block the site and warn others. Don't delete
        it or forward it to coworkers.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: evidence
      part: email
    evidence:
    - id: fake
      title: It was a fake page
      whyItMatters: The domain wasn't your company's.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: stolen
      title: Your password is stolen
      whyItMatters: Change it fast so it becomes useless.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: evidence
      title: The email is evidence
      whyItMatters: Reporting it lets IT limit the damage.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Say nothing, since nothing bad has happened yet
    - Forward the email to coworkers to ask if they got it too
    - Delete the email so no one finds out
    - Change your password on the official site now, report it to IT, and watch for
      sign-ins you didn't make
    correctChoice: 3
    feedback: Report it! Mistakes happen. <b>Hiding them</b> is what makes them worse.
    curriculumRef: "Social and Professional Issues (BIT2105). Supporting: IAS Week
      16 (incident reporting procedures) \u2014 Weeks 13\u201314: Security Incidents
      (\"recommend appropriate preventive and corrective measures\")"
    hint: If your password may be stolen, what makes the stolen one useless, and
      who needs to know?
    activity:
      kind: 2
      title: Account verified
      instruction: Check what happened, then choose your next step.
      items:
      - key: addr
        role: 0
        label: Address
        text: https://company-portal.login-verify.example/signin
      - key: done
        role: 5
        label: SIGNED IN
        text: Thank you! Your account has been verified.
      - key: typed
        role: 8
        label: 
        text: One minute ago you typed your work password on this page.
      - key: email
        role: 6
        label: inbox
        text: The email that sent you to this page
      minClues: 1
      outcomes:
      - The attacker can sign in as you while nobody knows. The longer it stays hidden,
        the worse the damage.
      - Coworkers open the same dangerous link, and more passwords are stolen.
      - IT loses the evidence it needs to block the site, and your stolen password
        still works.
      - The stolen password stops working, IT blocks the site and warns others, and
        you'll spot any login you didn't start.
  - id: M06
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Multiple Choice
    body: A classmate's school account password was stolen through phishing. <b>Which
      protection would most likely stop the attacker from logging in?</b>
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: <b>MFA</b> (multi-factor authentication) asks for a second check,
      like a code or an app prompt, as well as the password. So a stolen password
      alone isn't enough. The other choices don't stop someone logging in from far
      away. Only approve prompts you started yourself.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - A stronger Wi-Fi password at home
    - A privacy screen protector
    - Multi-factor authentication (MFA) on the account
    - Clearing the browser history
    correctChoice: 2
    feedback: MFA! A stolen password alone isn't enough, as long as you <b>never
      approve a prompt you didn't start</b>.
    curriculumRef: "Information Assurance in Security 1 (BIT1101) \u2014 Week 15:
      Security Controls and Protection Mechanisms (\"administrative, technical, and
      physical controls\")"
    hint: The attacker already has the password. What extra check would they still
      be missing?
    activity:
      kind: 0
      title: 
      instruction: 
      items: []
      minClues: 1
      outcomes: []
  - id: M07
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Scenario-Based
    body: "At a caf\xE9 you see two networks: <b>Kape_FreeWiFi</b> and <b>Kape_Free_WiFi</b>.
      After you connect to one, a page says: \"Log in with your SocialBook email
      and password to get internet access.\""
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: 'Two networks with almost the same name can mean one is a fake hotspot
      (an "evil twin"). No Wi-Fi needs your <i>social media</i> password: that page
      is stealing it. A VPN wouldn''t help, because you''d still be typing your password
      into a fake page.'
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: twin1
      field: 5
      match: Kape_FreeWiFi
      label: Network 1
      finding: Almost the same name as the other network. One of them may be a fake
        'evil twin' hotspot.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: twin
      part: w1
    - id: twin2
      field: 5
      match: Kape_Free_WiFi
      label: Network 2
      finding: Only an underscore apart. You can't tell which one is real without
        asking the staff.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: twin
      part: w2
    - id: portal
      field: 5
      match: SocialBook email and password
      label: The sign-in page
      finding: A Wi-Fi page has no reason to ask for a different site's password.
        That is credential harvesting.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: harvest
      part: portal
    - id: button
      field: 5
      match: LOG IN WITH SOCIALBOOK
      label: The button
      finding: 'Simulated: nothing is sent. A VPN wouldn''t help here - it can''t
        protect a password you type into a fake page.'
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: harvest
      part: button
    evidence:
    - id: twin
      title: Two lookalike networks
      whyItMatters: A sign of an 'evil twin' hotspot.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: harvest
      title: Asks for another site's password
      whyItMatters: Credential harvesting.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Don't enter it. Ask staff for the real network, and use mobile data for anything
      private
    - "Enter it, since caf\xE9s often ask you to log in"
    - Try both networks until one works without a login
    - Turn off your firewall so the network connects faster
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: Fake hotspot! No Wi-Fi needs your <b>social media password</b>. Ask
      staff, or use mobile data.
    curriculumRef: "Computer Security (BIT1225) \u2014 Network Security (\"firewalls,
      intrusion detection/prevention systems (IDS/IPS), and VPNs\")"
    hint: Two networks with almost the same name, and a page asking for a social
      media password. Does real Wi-Fi need that?
    activity:
      kind: 7
      title: "Wi-Fi  \xB7  networks nearby"
      instruction: Check the networks and the sign-in page, then choose.
      items:
      - key: w1
        role: 6
        label: open
        text: Kape_FreeWiFi
      - key: w2
        role: 6
        label: "open  \xB7  connected"
        text: Kape_Free_WiFi
      - key: portal
        role: 1
        label: Network sign-in page
        text: Log in with your SocialBook email and password to get internet access.
      - key: button
        role: 7
        label: 
        text: LOG IN WITH SOCIALBOOK
      minClues: 1
      outcomes:
      - Your SocialBook password stays safe. Staff point you to the real network,
        and you use mobile data for anything sensitive.
      - The fake hotspot collects your SocialBook login, and the attacker can take
        over your account.
      - You keep joining networks you can't trust, and anything sent on a fake one
        can be watched.
      - Your computer loses its protection on an untrusted network, and the fake
        page is still waiting for a password.
  - id: M08
    channel: 0
    sender: ExampleBank
    senderDetail: In the same thread as your real ExampleBank messages
    subject: 
    body: 'ExampleBank: Your account will be suspended TODAY. Update your details
      at'
    link: examplebank-ph.example/update
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 'Trusted domain (case files): examplebank.example'
    explanation: 'Scammers can fake the sender name on a text, so it can land in
      your real bank''s chat. Look at the link instead: <b>examplebank-ph.example</b>
      isn''t the bank''s real site, and "suspended TODAY" is pressure. Many banks
      don''t send links by text at all.'
    trigger: 3
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: thread
      field: 1
      match: 
      label: The thread
      finding: Scammers can fake the sender name so a text lands in your real bank's
        thread. The thread proves nothing.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: spoof
      part: 
    - id: today
      field: 3
      match: suspended TODAY
      label: Deadline
      finding: A same-day threat pushes you to act before you check.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: urgent
      part: 
    - id: request
      field: 3
      match: Update your details at
      label: The request
      finding: Banks don't ask you to update your details through a text link.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: request
      part: 
    - id: link
      field: 4
      match: 
      label: Link
      finding: examplebank-ph.example is not examplebank.example (the trusted domain
        in your case files).
      linkAddress: examplebank-ph.example/update
      clueId: domain
      part: 
    evidence:
    - id: spoof
      title: Sender name can be faked
      whyItMatters: Even in your real bank's thread.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: domain
      title: Wrong domain
      whyItMatters: examplebank-ph.example isn't your bank.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: urgent
      title: Same-day threat
      whyItMatters: Pressure to act now.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: request
      title: Asks for your details by link
      whyItMatters: Banks don't do that.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices: []
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: 'Scam! Sender names can be <b>faked</b>. Check the link: it''s not
      examplebank.example.'
    curriculumRef: "Cyber Forensics (BIT2226) \u2014 Mobile Device Forensics"
    hint: 'Ignore the sender name. Look at the link: is it the bank''s trusted address?'
    activity:
      kind: 0
      title: 
      instruction: 
      items: []
      minClues: 1
      outcomes: []
